{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And I will come in to her.</b> The Ramban wrote: this is not the way of propriety, even among the righteous. Rather, Scripture says ואבואה אליה — not that she should be given to me and I go my way, but that I would marry her and complete the few days that are upon me, for from now on she need not fear me — that I might leave you and abandon the livestock that are yours. End of quotation." "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain that this utterance by Jacob was not from the side of desire, in which there is corruption of ethics, but from the side of reason (sekhel). And know that according to reason there is no distinction between the organs of intercourse and the rest of the organs, such as the face and the hands. And never did a man and his wife make for themselves belts to cover the flesh of nakedness, until they ate and drew force from the Tree of Knowledge and became invested with desires. And since this is so, the patriarchs were rational beings like the ministering angels — there is nothing to wonder at in this matter, for their entire intention in using the instruments of the body was to be drawn after reason. Also the matriarchs, Rachel and Leah, who competed with one another over this natural act — all their words were of this sort, for they knew by ru'aḥ ha-kodesh that the twelve tribes were destined to come forth from Jacob through four women. And this is what Leah said to Rachel: ״המעט קחתך את אישי״ and Rachel answered: ״לכן ישכב עמך הלילה״, and Leah who went out to meet him and said to him ״אלי תבא כי שכור שכרתיך״ — their intention was not the pursuit of desire, heaven forbid, but the intention of the mitzvah alone, as they knew through prophecy. And this utterance is like prophecy among the patriarchs, whose every deed is drawn after reason, and all their words — even their casual conversation — upon it they encamp and upon it they journey; and likewise among the matriarchs, the prophetesses, there is in them no ugliness and no corruption of ethics. And a clear proof for this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: Rachel, who treated lightly the bed of the righteous, was not privileged to be buried with him; Leah, who demanded and said to him ״אלי תבא כי שכר שכרתיך״, was rewarded by having Issachar emerge from her — and this is ״וישכב עמה בלילה הוא״; it does not say בלילה ההוא but בלילה הוא, because הוא is the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He. And this is what Leah said: ״נתן אלהים שכרי״ — that is, my reward according to my intention, in the language of (Isaiah 40:10–11) ״הנה שכרו אתו ופעולתו לפניו״. And furthermore, from what is written ״וישמע אלהים אל לאה״ it is indicated that she prayed to the Holy One, blessed be He. From this you learn that concerning this natural act one was punished and the other was rewarded, and reward and punishment are only for the performance of mitzvot. And so all the matters indicate and testify that their intention was for a mitzvah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:26.527688317Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}